    "content": "MOGADISHU — In a public address on Tuesday, Somalia's Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre sharply criticized international partners, stating they have a \"cultural problem\" and are stuck in a mindset from the country's transitional era. He argued that despite Somalia's progress in establishing a constitution and functioning government, foreign actors continue to try to dictate policy, which he said is unacceptable.\n\nBarre emphasized that Somalia is no longer a failed state without laws or governance, and that international partners must adjust their approach accordingly. He called for a partnership based on equality and mutual respect, rather than one where external actors impose conditions and expectations. The prime minister's comments signal a growing assertiveness from Mogadishu in dealing with its international allies.\n\nThis rebuke comes amid tensions over international demands related to political inclusivity, electoral reforms, and security arrangements. Some international partners have expressed concerns over the pace of political agreements and have linked aid to these issues. The prime minister's remarks suggest frustration that such concerns are being addressed in a manner inconsistent with Somalia's current status.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this speech highlights the evolving relationship between Somalia and its international donors. It underscores a shift toward greater national ownership of political processes, which many in the diaspora have long advocated for. However, it also raises questions about the future of international cooperation and funding, which remain crucial for Somalia's stability and development.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-pm-hamza-abdi-barre-rebukes-international-partners/",
